	<key>readme</key>
	<string># TextPaster

Lists text presets for the frontmost app, with global presets appended. Select one to paste it; your previous clipboard is restored afterwards.

Alfred clears hotkeys when it imports a workflow, so set your own first: double-click the Hotkey object above and press your combination. ⌃⇧V is the suggested default. The `tp` keyword works either way.

Avoid a hotkey containing ⌘ if you use a remote-desktop client such as ScreenConnect — the Command key reaches the remote machine as the Windows key and opens the Start menu.

Keywords:
- `tp` — same as the hotkey: list presets for the frontmost app
- `tpconfig` — open the YAML config file
- `tpadd` — scaffold the frontmost app into the config (adds its bundle ID and name), then open the file

Config lives at `~/Library/Application Support/Alfred/Workflow Data/net.nightblade.textpaster/config.yaml` and is created on first use. Schema is documented in the file's header comments.

Per-app option `method: keystrokes` types the text via simulated key presses instead of pasting (clipboard untouched) — for apps that block or mangle ⌘V. Newlines are sent as real Return keypresses; characters not reachable on your keyboard layout may not type correctly.

Workflow variables (Alfred's [x] panel):
- `textpaster_paste_delay` (seconds, default 0.3) — how long the pasted text stays on the clipboard before the original clipboard is restored; raise it if a slow (e.g. Electron) app pastes stale content.
- `textpaster_editor` — app name to open the config with (e.g. `Visual Studio Code`); empty uses the system default handler for .yaml files.

Notes:
- Alfred needs Accessibility (it normally already has it); a one-time "control System Events" prompt may appear on first paste.
- Bundles js-yaml 4.1.0 (MIT, https://github.com/nodeca/js-yaml); see js-yaml.LICENSE.
- TextPaster itself is MIT licensed.</string>
